---
allowed-tools: Bash(git:*), Bash(chezmoi:*), Bash(pixi:*), Read, Glob, Grep
description: Sync chezmoi dotfiles — pull, apply, update tools{{ if .identity }}, push{{ end }}. One command to stay in sync everywhere.
---

## Context

- Chezmoi source dir: !`chezmoi source-path`
- Current machine state: !`chezmoi status 2>&1 | head -20`
- Source repo status: !`chezmoi git -- status --short`
- Current branch: !`chezmoi git -- branch --show-current`

This file has regressed twice on the rule below. Do not undo it:

**Never hardcode a `/home/<user>` path.** This repo is applied under several
different usernames. Run git against the source dir via `chezmoi git -- <args>`,
which resolves the source dir on whatever machine this is — and which stays inside
the already-granted `Bash(chezmoi:*)` permission.
{{ if not .identity }}
This machine has no git identity (`identity = false`), so this command is
**read-only toward the remote**: no `re-add`, no commit, no push. Landing changes
upstream is a separate, deliberate act performed from a machine that has one.
(Completing an interrupted rebase in step 2 is still fine — that is local history
only and writes nothing to the remote.)
{{ end }}
## Goal

Bring this machine's dotfiles up to date with remote `main`: pull, apply, and
reconcile installed tools.{{ if .identity }} Then push local changes back, so the
sync is bidirectional.{{ else }} Upstream wins on conflict.{{ end }}

## Steps

Execute these steps **in order**. Stop and report if any step fails in a way that
can't be auto-resolved.
{{ if .identity }}
### 1. Capture local drift, then commit

Detect files edited directly in `~` but not yet in the source dir:

```
chezmoi diff
```

If there are diffs, run `chezmoi re-add` to pull those changes back into the source
dir. Report what was re-added. Then:

- `chezmoi git -- add -A`
- If there are staged changes, commit with a concise message describing what changed
  (e.g. `chezmoi git -- commit -m "Update bash aliases and claude settings"`).
- If nothing to commit, skip.
{{ else }}
### 1. Report local drift (do not re-add)

Show what has drifted in `~` and in the source dir, so the user can see what the
apply in step 3 will overwrite:

```
chezmoi diff
chezmoi git -- status --short
```

Do **not** run `chezmoi re-add`, and do **not** commit. If drift looks like something
the user would not want overwritten, stop and ask before continuing.
{{ end }}
### 2. Pull remote changes

```
chezmoi git -- pull --rebase --autostash origin main
```

`--autostash` preserves uncommitted local edits across the pull — but only when
upstream does not touch the same files. If upstream has modified a file that also has
local edits, the local version is lost; call that out explicitly in the report.

- **If rebase succeeds cleanly:** continue.
- **If there are merge conflicts:**
  1. List the conflicting files.
  2. For each conflict, read the file and resolve it sensibly — prefer the version
     that adds more content; if ambiguous, keep both sides and flag it to the user.
  3. `chezmoi git -- add <files>` for the resolved files, then
     `chezmoi git -- rebase --continue`.
  4. Report every resolution made so the user can verify.

### 3. Apply to home directory

```
chezmoi apply --force
```

This updates `~` from the (now up-to-date) source dir.

`--force` rewrites every managed file with no prompt, including `~/.gitconfig`. If
the `chezmoi diff` from step 1 showed a `credential` section being **removed** from
`.gitconfig`, that is a `gh auth setup-git` helper about to be destroyed — it belongs
in the untracked `~/.gitconfig.local`. Say so in the report and re-add it there; do
not silently let the apply drop it. (See the README troubleshooting entry "git stops
authenticating over HTTPS after a /sync".)

If `.chezmoi.toml.tmpl` changed in the pull, run `chezmoi init` **before** applying,
or templates fail with `map has no entry for key "profile"`. Keep the existing
profile: `CHEZMOI_PROFILE=$(chezmoi execute-template '{{ "{{" }} .profile {{ "}}" }}') chezmoi init`.

Read the resolved profile back *before* the pull, in case the pull changes the template.

### 4. Sync & update installed tools

First update pixi itself, so the manifest is read by the newest resolver:

```
pixi self-update
```

Report the version change, or that it was already current. If this fails (e.g. pixi
was installed by a package manager rather than its own installer), report the failure
and continue with the remaining steps — it is not fatal.

The applied manifest (`dot_pixi/manifests/pixi-global.toml.tmpl` →
`~/.pixi/manifests/pixi-global.toml`) may have added, removed, or changed pixi global
packages. Next reconcile the installed tools with the manifest:

```
pixi global sync
```

Then upgrade every installed package to the newest version allowed by the manifest:

```
pixi global update
```

Report any tools installed, removed, or updated. (Note: `pixi global list` can show
an env as present while its binary was never exposed to `~/.pixi/bin` —
`pixi global sync` fixes this, so always run it even if `list` looks correct.)
{{ if .identity }}
### 5. Push

```
chezmoi git -- push origin main
```

If push is rejected (remote has new commits since our pull), pull-rebase again and
retry once.
{{ end }}
### Report

Print a short summary:
{{ if .identity }}- Files re-added from local drift (if any)
- Commits made locally
{{ end }}- Commits pulled from remote
- Conflicts resolved (if any)
- Pixi version change from `pixi self-update` (if any)
- Tools synced via pixi (installed/removed/updated, if any)
- Final status: **in sync** or **action needed** (with details)
{{ if not .identity }}
Uncommitted local edits remain uncommitted. Say so, rather than implying the machine
and the remote are now identical.
{{ end }}
